<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julia Ward Howe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Julia Ward</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julia Ward Howe was an American author, poet and social activist who wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a> as new words to the song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a>. She wrote them in November 1861 after visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, and meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>; published in the Atlantic Monthly in February 1862, the song became one of the most popular of the Union during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. She held that freeing the slaves was right but did not believe in racial equality. After the war she worked for pacifism and women's suffrage, co-leading the American Woman Suffrage Association with Lucy Stone from 1869, and her Mothers' Day Proclamation called on women around the world to unite for peace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julia-ward-howe.jpg" alt="Julia Ward Howe" width="307" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JuliaWardHowe_2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Julia Ward Howe</b>, an abolitionist writer, heard troops singing the song at a review outside Washington, D.C., on Munson's Hill near Bailey's Crossroads, Virginia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The most famous is <b>Julia Ward Howe</b>'s &quot;Battle Hymn of the Republic&quot;, written in November 1861 when a friend asked her for good words for that stirring tune; Kimball suggests that the friend was President Abraham Lincoln.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-rhode-island">Portsmouth</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1878" aria-label="New York City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2736 710 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julia Ward Howe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3186 980h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3186 980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1878">What was here in 1878? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
